"""NGS GEOID18 heights at anchoring-discriminating positions. WP-G1 anchors.

Every geoid height below was computed by the National Geodetic Survey's own
geoid height service and captured verbatim from its API:

    https://geodesy.noaa.gov/api/geoid/ght?lat=<lat>&lon=<lon>&model=14

where model 14 is GEOID18. Captured 2026-08-07 by
review/wp-v4-anchoring/decide_geoid_anchoring.py, frozen from its committed
output review/wp-v4-anchoring/geoid18_120_discriminating_points.json.
**No test touches the network.**

**Why a second anchor set exists beside ``geoid_anchors.GEOID_ANCHORS``.** The
20 original anchors cannot tell the two candidate stencil anchorings apart:
every scheme lands inside NGS's own 0.001 m printing quantization on them, so
the geoid suite passed with ``geoid_height`` anchored either way - a pin that
had stopped discriminating (DESIGN.md #31's class, found by seeding exactly
that defect at the WP-V4 gate; DESIGN.md #36). These positions were chosen the
opposite way: at fractional cell position 0.9 in both directions - where the
floor-anchored and nearest-node stencils are guaranteed to differ - inside the
120 highest-curvature Michigan cells, where a quadratic stencil placement
matters most. Selection used the grid alone; the truth values are NGS's, so the
comparison is external (DESIGN.md #36 records why this is good discrimination
and NOT an unbiased estimate of typical error - for that, see the 20 anchors).

``discriminates=True`` marks the 36 positions where the two anchorings disagree
about NGS's printed figure itself: the nearest-node (INTG) stencil rounds to
NGS's value and the floor-anchored one does not. Derived once from the
committed tile when this fixture was frozen, and falsified: with
``geoid_height`` seeded back to the floor anchoring, all 36 fail. The honest
remainder: at 19 of the 120 the floor anchoring rounds to NGS and nearest-node
does not - printing-boundary noise, not a countertrend - and the aggregate is
83/120 against 66/120 in nearest-node's favour, rms 0.454 mm against 0.715
(DESIGN.md #36, reproduced independently at the WP-G1 gate).

NGS prints geoid heights to 0.001 m, so a published figure carries +/-0.0005 m
of quantization on its own. Every value is negative: the ellipsoid is above
the geoid throughout the conterminous United States.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

from dataclasses import dataclass


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class DiscriminatingAnchor:
    """One NGS geoid height at a position chosen to expose the anchoring."""

    latitude: float
    longitude: float
    """Decimal degrees, negative west. Fractional cell position 0.9."""

    geoid_height_m: float
    """NGS's printed figure, 0.001 m resolution, verbatim."""

    discriminates: bool
    """True where nearest-node rounds to NGS's figure and floor does not."""


